# `terminate` must stop an *internal* job's underlying execution, not just flip its
# status flag -- confirmed here by backgrounding a two-statement block (`{sleep ...;
# cmd touch $marker} &`), terminating it while the first statement (a long `sleep`) is
# still running, and then confirming the second statement (which would create
# `$marker`) never actually ran.
#
# Detecting "did the second statement run" via a real file-touch side effect, not by
# scanning the pty screen for text, after this exact approach was reviewed and found
# broken three independent ways:
# 1. A glob pattern's `*` doesn't match across `/` -- every pty screen also contains the
#    prompt's own cwd path, so a `*MARKER*`-style glob against the *whole* screen can
#    read as not-matching regardless of what the backgrounded job actually did, purely
#    because of an unrelated `/` elsewhere on screen.
# 2. The line a human/script *types* into the session is itself echoed back onto the
#    screen by the pty's own line discipline -- so if the second statement's own marker
#    text appears literally in the *typed* `{...} &` command line (as it must, to name
#    what the statement should do), a screen-text search would trivially "find" it from
#    that echo alone, regardless of whether the statement ever actually ran.
# 3. Even independent of both of the above, a backgrounded job's own output in this
#    exact shape did not reliably reach the rendered pty screen at all in practice.
# A side effect with its own persistent, independently-observable state (a file either
# existing or not, checked from *this* script, entirely outside the pty session) has
# none of these problems.
#
# This is deliberately *not* the "status flag" scenario `tests/vt_terminate_and_signal_
# external_job.crush`/Task 9 already cover (those are about an *external* `cmd` job's
# `jobs` row correctly reading `Terminated`). The narrower bug this test guards against
# is real, found and fixed in this same task: `GlobalState::terminate` already marks the
# targeted job (and every job nested inside it -- see `descendant_job_ids`) `Terminated`
# synchronously, and `sleep` itself (being cooperative -- see `tests/terminate.crush`)
# does stop its own underlying wait almost immediately. But `Closure::eval_inner`'s
# sequential-statement loop used to decide whether to advance to the *next* statement
# purely by checking whether `ThreadStore::join_job` returned an error for the one that
# just ran -- and that join is not race-free (two independent races; see
# `JobHandle::termination_requested`'s own doc comment in `src/lang/state/handles.rs`
# for both). Not reproducible on every single run through this exact pty-driven,
# interactive-session timing specifically -- seen directly: running this file's own
# scenario (file-touch check included) against a deliberately-reverted build showed
# the marker file wrongly created on roughly half of a batch of repeated runs, not
# every one -- but genuinely reproducible, and the point of this test is to keep it
# caught, not to pin down its exact odds.
#
# Job id `0` for the backgrounded block: confirmed directly against the debug binary
# (dumping `$t:contents` after the first `jobs` call) -- in a freshly created pty
# session, the first job ever typed gets id 0, same precedent as the sibling pty
# job-control tests (e.g. `pty_bg_resumes_without_foregrounding.crush`).
#
# The marker path is unique per run (suffixed with this *outer* script's own pid, via
# `crush:pid`, not the nested session's) so concurrent test runs can never collide on
# the same file. A best-effort delete of any stale marker from an earlier run happens
# before the check below for the same reason pids get reused at all: a *previous* run
# of this exact test, under a pid that's since been recycled, could have left this
# same path behind (e.g. if that earlier run's own assertion below had failed, since a
# failed `assert` ends the script before `$t:close` -- there's nothing to a "finally"
# here) -- and an already-existing file would wrongly read back as "the second
# statement ran" on *this* run before it ever got a chance to actually create it.
#

# A settle delay follows each `wait_for "# "` below, same precedent as the sibling pty
# tests (e.g. `pty_terminate_and_signal_external_job.crush`): the prompt text is part of
# the same rendered line a freshly-typed command appears on, so a bare `wait_for "# "`
# can be satisfied by a prompt already on screen before the write actually lands.
$crush_bin := $(crush:env["CRUSH_BIN"])
$my_pid := $(crush:pid)
$marker := $("/tmp/vt_terminate_internal_job_marker_{}":format $my_pid)
try {
    cmd rm "-f" $marker
} catch {
}
$cmd_line := $("{{sleep $(duration:of seconds=100); cmd touch \"{}\"}} &\n":format $marker)

$t := $(pty:create $crush_bin)
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)

$t:write $cmd_line
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)

$t:write "terminate 0\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)

$t:write "jobs\n"
$t:wait_for "Terminated"
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=1500)

$marker_exists := $true
try {
    fs:stat $marker
} catch {
    $marker_exists = $false
}
assert (!$marker_exists) "terminate must actually stop the backgrounded block's underlying execution, not just flip its status -- the second statement must never run"

$t:close
echo "ok"
